--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Reading one test closely: the "touches kernel memory" check
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

kernel/src/trustselftest.rs:350-366:

    let mem_exit = proc::run_user_process(pid2);
    let mem_faulted = match mem_exit {
        proc::ProcessExitReason::Fault { vector, cr2, .. } => {
            vector == 14 && cr2 == 0xffff_8000_0000_0000
        }
        _ => false,
    };
    check(mem_faulted, "ring 3: process touching kernel memory faults (#PF) ...",
          "bad_mem did not fault on kernel memory");

Left: the exit reason the kernel stored after handling the fault.
Right: vector 14 and cr2 equal to a constant written in the same file
(kernel/src/proc.rs:596, `mov rax, 0xffff800000000000`).

Both sides come from the code under test, so by AGENTS.md rule 1 this check
cannot fail for the reason it claims to test unless the fault never happens. It
does not even assert the page-fault error code: `code` is destructured away with
`..`. The error code is the part that separates "this page is protected" from
"this address happens to be unmapped", and those are very different claims.

The committed run says which one it is:

    proc: PID 2 (bad_mem) killed by fault: vector=14 code=0x4 rip=0x40000a cr2=0xffff800000000000

x86-64 page-fault error code bit 0 (P) = 0 means the failing entry is NOT PRESENT;
bit 2 (U/S) = 1 means the access came from user mode. So 0x4 = "user read of an
address with no mapping". It is not a supervisor page being protected.

Measured with a review-only probe in the scratch clone (a fourth user process
reading 0xffffffff80000000, which the kernel PML4 does map), same build, same
boot:

    REVIEW_PROBE read_kernel_text=Fault { vector: 14, code: 5, rip: 4194311, cr2: 18446744071562067968 }
    (cr2 = 0xffffffff80000000; code 5 = P=1, U/S=1 -> a real protection fault)

So the isolation the author built is real, but the committed check would pass on
an unmapped address whatever the page-table flags were. That is exactly the
"plausible scaffolding around an unverified claim" failure AGENTS.md exists to
prevent.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Is the Ring 3 entry real? CS/SS from hardware, not from a flag the test sets
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Yes, with evidence. `proc::run_user_process` (kernel/src/proc.rs) iretqs with
SS=0x33 and CS=0x3B; the GDT descriptors are DPL=3 (kernel/src/gdt.rs), IDT gate
0x80 is DPL=3 (`type_attr = 0xEE`, kernel/src/idt.rs set_gate_user), and
`common_exc` routes on the hardware-pushed CS (`test byte ptr [rsp + 40], 3`).

I did not take that on trust. In the scratch clone I read the CS the CPU itself
pushed on the first `int 0x80` frame (syscall_entry: hardware frame sits above
the 14 saved GPRs, CS at [rsp+120]) and printed it:

    REVIEW_SYSCALL frame_cs=0x3b cpl=3 num=3

0x3B & 3 = 3, read from hardware state, in the process that carries the syscall.
The `cli` #GP (vector 13 at user rip 0x400000) and the kernel-text #PF code 5
independently confirm CPL=3.

Weakness F2 below: no committed test asserts that value. The suite detects a
ring-0 "user" process only indirectly (plant B, section 4), and only because the
iretq itself becomes invalid; a future refactor that entered ring 0 cleanly would
not be caught by any of the 31 checks.

