4. Plants (not the author's)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Plant A — a real kernel-memory leak the suite does not see.
Review-only: after `spawn_user` maps the two user pages, set U/S on every level of
the translation path to kernel text 0xffffffff80000000 in that process's PML4
(scratch clone, `review_make_user_reachable`, switch REVIEW_PLANT_TEXT_U).
The 31 committed checks were not edited.

    $ bash kernel/scripts/test-boot.sh
    --- SeaBIOS/selftest: exit=33 serial tail:
        nova> PASS (SeaBIOS/selftest)
    --- OVMF/selftest: exit=33 serial tail:
        nova> PASS (OVMF/selftest)

    serial log:
      ok    ring 3: process touching kernel memory faults (#PF) and dies while kernel continues
      REVIEW_PROBE read_kernel_text=Exited(0)          <-- ring 3 process read kernel text
      nova> NOVA_SELFTEST_OK

A ring 3 process read the kernel's own text page and exited cleanly; all 31 checks
stayed green and the selftest exited 33. The suite is green with a live isolation
hole. By the reviewer's rule in the queue message, that is a FAIL.

Plant B — the suite does notice a bogus ring-3 transition.
Review-only: push CS=0x38 (RPL 0) instead of 0x3B in `enter_user_mode`.

    $ bash kernel/scripts/test-boot.sh
    --- SeaBIOS/selftest: exit=124 serial tail:
    FAIL (SeaBIOS/selftest)
    --- OVMF/selftest: exit=124 serial tail:
    FAIL (OVMF/selftest)

    serial log tail:
    NOVA_FAULT: vector 13 at rip=0xffffffff8000185f
      error code: 0x38
    NOVA: halted.

Red, but by halting the kernel inside `iretq`, not by a failed check: the run
times out (124) instead of exiting 35. Worth knowing for diagnosis — a bad user
transition is reported as a hang, and nothing in the transcript names the cause.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. The queue's six questions
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(1) Is the Ring 3 entry real (CS/SS from hardware state, not a flag)?
    YES. frame_cs=0x3b, cpl=3 read from the hardware-pushed frame (section 3);
    cli #GP and kernel-text #PF code 5 corroborate. No committed test asserts
    CPL, though (F2).

(2) Can a process reach a kernel page through any user-mapped table?
    NO as committed, YES under plant A. Runtime walk of every process PML4 in the
    scratch clone:

      REVIEW_DUMP kernel_pml4[256] present=1 user=0 writable=1 addr=0x1d7fb000
      REVIEW_DUMP kernel_pml4[510] present=1 user=0 writable=1 addr=0x1ff84000
      REVIEW_DUMP kernel_pml4[511] present=1 user=0 writable=1 addr=0x1ff9e000
      REVIEW_DUMP pid=1 cr3=0x1cffa000
      REVIEW_DUMP   user_pml4[0]=0x1cff8007
      REVIEW_DUMP   leaf va=0x400000 pa=0x1cff9000 user=1 writable=0
      REVIEW_DUMP   leaf va=0x7ff000 pa=0x1cff5000 user=1 writable=1
      REVIEW_DUMP pid=1 leaf_count=2

    The process has exactly two reachable pages, both its own, code read-only and
    stack writable; the three inherited kernel PML4 entries are all U/S=0 and
    nothing below them is walked in the lower half. So the construction is sound,
    but the committed test does not establish it (section 2, plant A).

(3) Does the syscall path validate user pointers and registers?
    There are no user pointers. The ABI is three integers in registers
    (num, arg1, arg2 in rax/rdi/rsi; the stub shifts them to the SysV slots), and
    no syscall dereferences a user address, so no copy_from_user/to_user exists
    at all. arg1 is validated by `Resource::from_index` (unknown -> ERR_INVAL);
    arg2/arg3 are ignored. Registers are not otherwise checked, and they do not
    need to be for this ABI. This answers the question as asked, but the honest
    statement is "vacuously true": pointer validation is absent, not implemented.

(4) Is the kernel stack in the TSS per process?
    The stack is per process, the TSS is not. `spawn_user` allocates a 16 KiB
    kernel stack and stores its top in `proc.kstack_top`; `run_user_process` arms
    the single TSS with `gdt::set_tss_rsp0(proc.kstack_top)` before entering user
    mode. That is correct for one process at a time. It is NOT correct for a
    context switch: `proc::switch_to` (kernel/src/proc.rs) changes CR3 and
    CURRENT_PID but never touches TSS.rsp0, and the commit message and the
    sched.rs header both claim "TSS.rsp0 dynamic reloading on process switch".
    See F3.

(5) Does a fault in one process leave the others running?
    The kernel survives and keeps running: after each fault `handle_user_fault`
    marks the process Exited, logs the vector and returns through
    `return_to_kernel`, and the selftest continues to PID 3 and then PID 0. But no
    second process is runnable at the time, so "the others" is unproven: the three
    processes are run one after another by direct `run_user_process` calls, not by
    the scheduler, which has no callers at all (F3, F9).

(6) Is the capability table consulted on every syscall path?
    On every path that names a resource, yes: SYS_REQUEST_RESOURCE -> 
    `Resource::from_index` -> `cur.caps.grants(res)` -> GRANTED/DENIED, logged.
    SYS_YIELD and SYS_EXIT are not resource operations. The gate is deny-by-default
    and `CapSet::new()` is empty; the novad set is deserialised through
    `CapSet::from_manifest`, which refuses the reserved resource. Findings: the
    set comes from a hardcoded array in sched::init, not from a signed manifest
    (F6), and no real resource operation exists behind any capability yet (F7).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. Findings for the author (not fixed here)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

F1 (blocking, FAIL). The "touches kernel memory" check does not test kernel
    memory. kernel/src/proc.rs:596 probes 0xffff800000000000, which is unmapped in
    the process address space (error code 0x4), and
    kernel/src/trustselftest.rs:350-366 destructures only vector and cr2, so
    neither P nor U/S is checked. Plant A gives a ring 3 process read access to
    kernel text and keeps the suite green (exit 33). Fix: probe a mapped kernel
    page (e.g. 0xffffffff80000000) and assert vector 14, cr2 == that address AND
    error code & 1 == 1 (protection, not not-present); add a review process that
    must fault on it, and a plant that clears U/S along the kernel-text path must
    turn the suite red.

F2 (high). No committed test asserts CPL from hardware state. Ring 3 is proven
    only by consequences (cli #GP, unmapped kernel address #PF). Add an assertion
    on the hardware-pushed CS of a syscall frame (0x3B & 3 == 3), as measured in
    section 3, so the "ring 3" claim is checked directly.

F3 (high). `proc::switch_to` does not reload TSS.rsp0 and does not switch
    context; the commit message's "TSS.rsp0 dynamic reloading on process switch"
    and the identical claim in kernel/src/sched.rs are false for that function.
    Either make switch_to do it or remove the claim.

F4 (medium). Faulted and exited processes stay in state Exited forever; spawn
    searches for Unused only, so after 7 exits no new process can start
    (MAX_PROCS = 8). There is no reaper. A long-lived novad plus three test
    processes per boot never shows it.

F5 (low). `get_process_mut` returns Some for an Unused slot while `get_process`
    returns None; a caller can accidentally revive a free slot.

F6 (medium). `sched::init` builds novad's CapSet from a hardcoded array, not from
    a signed manifest, while kernel/src/proc.rs:9 and the sched comment say
    "granted by its signed manifest" / "from manifest". trust.rs still has no
    callers (the author is right to leave that for K4-T, but the comment is not
    what the code does).

F7 (medium). Every capability currently gates nothing but its own request: no
    kernel resource operation is reachable from a syscall, so "a process cannot
    touch a resource absent from its capability set" is proven for the token, not
    for the resource. When FsRead is exposed to novad, the enforcement point must
    move with it, and the test must attempt the real operation.

F8 (low). The self-test banner printed by this run still says
    "K4 in-kernel C2 trust and capability gate (per-process PML4, ring 0 only, no
    isolation yet)" (docs/logs/k4-r3-selftest.log:308) while the same run executes
    ring 3. Misleading output; AGENTS.md rule 10.

F9 (medium). The scheduler is dead code: nothing calls sched::schedule or
    sched::yield_now, and SYS_YIELD returns 0 without scheduling. The work order
    asked for a syscall path and novad as the first process, which exist; it did
    not ask for working scheduling (K4-T does), but sched.rs's module doc says
    "Cooperative & Preemptive Scheduler", which is not true today.

F10 (low). `handle_syscall` (proc.rs) is a second, unused copy of the dispatch
    logic, without the logging the real path has. Two mediation paths drift.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. What I could not do / am unsure of
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Could not: run the author's own three plants (I ran two of my own instead); run
under KVM (Windows QEMU TCG only); test with more than 7 processes (the slot leak
is derived by reading the table, not demonstrated by a run); verify the
OVMF-pflash path beyond test-boot.sh's own PASS line.

Unsure: whether the slot leak (F4) is reachable in the intended product flow,
where novad is the only long-lived process; whether the lack of pointer arguments
(question 3 in section 5) is an intentional K4 scope cut or simply not yet built; and
whether plant A's U-bit change (it also loosens the kernel's own tables, since the
upper levels are shared) overstates the leak a real bug would produce — the leak
is real and the suite did not notice it either way, but the plant is stronger than
a plausible accident.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERDICT: FAIL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ring 3 is real (hardware CS 0x3b, CPL 3), the capability gate denies and logs,
and the fault path keeps the kernel alive: those claims hold and reproduce. But
the suite does not prove the isolation claim it advertises — the "kernel memory"
probe reads an unmapped address with the error code discarded, and a plant giving
a ring 3 process read access to kernel text leaves all 31 checks green with exit
33 (F1). Reopen K4-R3 for F1 and F2 at minimum; F3/F4/F6/F7/F9 should be fixed or
explicitly deferred in the same commit.
